"answer": "Mitochondria are membrane-bound organelles in eukaryotic cells responsible for generating most of the chemical energy needed to power cellular biochemical reactions, primarily through the production of adenosine triphosphate (ATP) via cellular respiration. They also play a role in signaling, cellular differentiation, cell death, and maintaining control of the cell cycle and cell growth.",
"confidence": "high",
"key_points": [
"Generate ATP through cellular respiration",
"Act as the primary energy producer of the eukaryotic cell",
"Involved in apoptosis (programmed cell death)",
"Regulate cellular metabolism and signaling"
]
